%description:

RFC 5681 -- in slow start the window grows by at most one segment per acknowledgment.

Checks RFC5681-SS-2 (the upper bound); covers RFC5681-SS-1 and RFC5681-SSTH-1.

RFC 5681 section 3.1: while the congestion window is below the threshold, it may be
increased by at most SMSS bytes for each acknowledgment that covers new data. The rule is an
upper bound, and that is what this check reads.

**How the rule is observed.** The rule is about two consecutive publications of the window,
not about any one of them, so the check keeps the previous value and judges each increase
against it. The guard runs beside the transfer and forbids an increase larger than one
segment, so a single violation anywhere in the run fails the check. A step that looked at
one publication could not say this at all.

The segment size is taken from the run rather than assumed: the first data segment's length
is captured and used as the bound. That length includes the TCP header, so the bound is
larger than SMSS by about twenty bytes. The bound is therefore slightly generous, and a
model that grew the window by two segments would still fail it by a wide margin.

**One expected observation cannot be made.** The check also asks for the threshold to be
read before any loss. `TcpReno` publishes `ssthresh` only when it changes it, which happens
on a loss, so a run without loss publishes it never. This is the same gap the RFC 6298
checks met from the other side: the model publishes a control variable only where it
recomputes it, not where it is used.

Observation 3 of the check, that the window roughly doubles each round trip, is weaker than
the bound above and is not asserted. A model that grows more slowly meets the `MUST` and
misses the shape, which the check calls a deviation to record rather than a violation.

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

// The previous publication of the window. One program runs once per binary, so file scope
// is the state this rule needs; the QUIC flow-control check keeps its total the same way.
static double previousWindow = -1;

// True when this publication grew the window by more than one segment since the last one.
// The guard below forbids exactly that.
static bool grewByMoreThanOneSegment(const MatchContext& context)
{
    if (!context.event.hasValue)
        return false;
    double window = context.event.value;
    double oneSegment = context.get("segmentBytes").doubleValue();
    bool tooFast = previousWindow >= 0 && (window - previousWindow) > oneSegment;
    previousWindow = window;
    return tooFast;
}

static bool dataSegment(const MatchContext& context)
{
    return context.event.packet != nullptr && context.event.packet->getByteLength() > 100;
}

static cValue packetBytes(const PacketEvent& event)
{
    return cValue((double)event.packet->getByteLength());
}

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("rfc5681_slow_start_growth")
        // Take the segment size from the run. This is the bound the rule uses.
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .filterPacket("tcp.destPort == 1000 && tcp.synBit == false")
                  .filterEvent(dataSegment)
                  .capture("segmentBytes", packetBytes)
                  .describe("host A's first data segment, whose length is the bound")
                  .within(2.0))
        // From here to the end of the transfer, no acknowledgment may grow the window by
        // more than one segment.
        .meanwhile(never(on("host1.tcp").signal("cwnd")
                             .filterEvent(grewByMoreThanOneSegment)
                             .describe("a window that grew by more than one segment")
                             .within(8.0)))
        // The transfer really ran inside that window, so the guard held over something.
        // Several round trips of growth happen before this point.
        .once(on("host2.tcp").signal("packetReceivedFromLower")
                  .filterEvent(dataSegment).nth(20)
                  .describe("the twentieth data segment at host B")
                  .within(8.0));
}

%activity:
// present only so opp_test compiles the %global above; this module is not in the network.
EV << "rfc5681_slow_start_growth compiled\n";

%file: test.ned

import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import inet.node.ethernet.Eth100M;
import inet.networklayer.configurator.ipv4.Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;

// Two hosts on one link -- note: NO tester (attached at runtime).
network SlowStartNet
{
    submodules:
        configurator: Ipv4NetworkConfigurator { @display("p=100,50"); }
        host1: StandardHost { @display("p=300,100"); }
        host2: StandardHost { @display("p=500,100"); }
    connections:
        host1.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> host2.ethg++;
}

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
network = SlowStartNet
sim-time-limit = 20s
seed-set = 0

**.ipv4.arp.typename = "GlobalArp"

# A receiver window large enough never to limit the sender, so the congestion window is the
# only thing that decides how much is in flight.
**.tcp.advertisedWindow = 65535

*.host1.numApps = 1
*.host1.app[0].typename = "TcpSessionApp"
*.host1.app[0].connectAddress = "host2"
*.host1.app[0].connectPort = 1000
*.host1.app[0].tOpen = 0.1s
*.host1.app[0].tSend = 0.2s
# Enough data to fill several windows, so the growth is visible over several round trips.
*.host1.app[0].sendBytes = 100000B
*.host1.app[0].tClose = -1s
*.host2.numApps = 1
*.host2.app[0].typename = "TcpSinkApp"
*.host2.app[0].localPort = 1000

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST rfc5681_slow_start_growth: PASS
